{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-102328" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the stationary points of $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-6x-2y+12.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-102328" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x-6 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2y-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x-6 & 2y-2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-6=0 \\\\ 2y-2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x-6=0\\,⟹\\,x=3.\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $y$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n2y-2=0\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has one solution, $(3,1).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(3,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-102328" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a9fa983acf62cb4e9aa8bffc8cac999fd15910439357fbbe6843cbb905417b2c"} {"question" "q-102328" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-102328" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-1,3)"] ["math" "(1,3)"] ["math" "(3,1)"] ["text" "$(1,1)$ and $(3,1)$"] ["text" "$(1,3)$ and $(3,-1)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-102328" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(3,1)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-103925" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the stationary point of $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+xy-3x+3y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-103925" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x+y-3 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2y+x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x+y-3 & 2y+x+3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+y-3=0 \\\\ 2y+x+3=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n2x+y-3=0\\,⟹\\,y=3-2x.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(3-2x)+x+3 & =0 \\\\ 6-4x+x+3 & =0 \\\\ 9-3x & =0 \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting this value into $y=3-2x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=3-2(3)=-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has one solution, $(3,-3).$ \n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(3,-3).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-103925" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a9fa983acf62cb4e9aa8bffc8cac999fd15910439357fbbe6843cbb905417b2c"} {"question" "q-103925" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-103925" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-3,6)"] ["math" "(-3,9)"] ["math" "(3,-2)"] ["math" "(3,-3)"] ["math" "(6,-9)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-103925" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(3,-3)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-36622" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the stationary points of $f(x,y)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2xy+{y}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-36622" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is $D={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}∣x>0},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{2}{x}-2y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-2x+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x}-2y & -2x+2y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x}-2y=0 \\\\ -2x+2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{x}-2y=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+2(\\frac{1}{x}) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=\\frac{1}{x},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=-1 & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{(-1)}=-1 \\\\ x=1 & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1).$\nWe reject the point $(-1,-1)$ because it does not lie in the domain of $f(x,y).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-36622" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a9fa983acf62cb4e9aa8bffc8cac999fd15910439357fbbe6843cbb905417b2c"} {"question" "q-36622" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-36622" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(0,0)"] ["math" "(1,1)"] ["text" "$(-1,-1)$ and $(0,0)$"] ["text" "$(-1,1)$ and $(1,-1)$"] ["text" "$(1,1)$ and $(-1,-1)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-36622" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(1,1)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-80646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the stationary point of $f(x,y)=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-4xy+{y}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-80646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is $D={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}∣x>0},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =\\frac{2}{x}-4y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-4x+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x}-4y & -4x+2y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{x}-4y=0 \\\\ -4x+2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{x}-4y=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2x}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4x+2(\\frac{1}{2x}) & =0 \\\\ -4x+(\\frac{1}{x}) & =0 \\\\ 4x & =\\frac{1}{x}. \\\\ x & =\\pm \\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=\\frac{1}{2x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{1}{2} & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2(\\frac{1}{2})}=1 \\\\ x=-\\frac{1}{2} & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{1}{2(-\\frac{1}{2})}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(\\frac{1}{2},1)$ and $(-\\frac{1}{2},-1).$ We reject the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},-1)$ because it does not lie in the domain of $f(x,y).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(\\frac{1}{2},1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-80646" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a9fa983acf62cb4e9aa8bffc8cac999fd15910439357fbbe6843cbb905417b2c"} {"question" "q-80646" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-80646" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(-\\frac{1}{2},-1)"] ["math" "(-\\frac{1}{2},1)"] ["math" "(\\frac{1}{2},-1)"] ["math" "(\\frac{1}{2},1)"] ["math" "(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-80646" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(\\frac{1}{2},1)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-80685" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the critical points of $f(x,y)={x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}y+6{y}^{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-80685" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =3{x}^{2}-6xy \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-3{x}^{2}+12y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6xy & -3{x}^{2}+12y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\n\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6xy=0 \\\\ -3{x}^{2}+12y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $y$ in the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n-3{x}^{2}+12y=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6x(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-\\frac{6{x}^{3}}{4} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}(1-\\frac{x}{2}) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}(2-x) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=0 & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{{0}^{2}}{4}=0 \\\\ x=2 & \\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{{2}^{2}}{4}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(0,0)$ and $(2,1).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has two critical points, $(0,0)$ and $(2,1).$ Note that since ${f}^{′}(x)={0}^{T}$ at these two points, they are also both stationary points." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-80685" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a9fa983acf62cb4e9aa8bffc8cac999fd15910439357fbbe6843cbb905417b2c"} {"question" "q-80685" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-80685" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(-2,0)$ and $(2,1)$"] ["text" "$(0,-2)$ and $(1,1)$"] ["text" "$(0,0)$ and $(1,2)$"] ["text" "$(0,0)$ and $(2,1)$"] ["text" "$(0,1)$ and $(2,-1)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-80685" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(0,0)$ and $(2,1)$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-104995" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3y & 3{y}^{2}-3x\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(1,1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $H(1,1)>0$\n- $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)<0$\n- The point $(1,1)$ is a local maximum of $f$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-104995" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-3y & 3{y}^{2}-3x\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}-3y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=3{y}^{2}-3x.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{x}^{2}-3y)=6x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3{y}^{2}-3x)=6y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3{y}^{2}-3x)=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}6x & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=[\\begin{aligned}6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=det({f}^{″}(1,1))={6}^{2}-(-3)^{2}=27>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)=({f}^{″}(1,1))_{11}=6>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(1,1)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)>0,$ the point $(1,1)$ is a local minimum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-104995" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a9fa983acf62cb4e9aa8bffc8cac999fd15910439357fbbe6843cbb905417b2c"} {"question" "q-104995" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-104995" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-104995" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-104984" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}(2{x}^{2}-1) & 2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} \\\\ 2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} & {e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}(2{y}^{2}-1)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(0,0).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $H(0,0)>0$\n- $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)>0$\n- The point $(0,0)$ is a local maximum of $f$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-104984" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=(-1)^{2}-{0}^{2}=1>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,0))_{11}=-1<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(0,0)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)<0,$ the point $(0,0)$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-104984" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a9fa983acf62cb4e9aa8bffc8cac999fd15910439357fbbe6843cbb905417b2c"} {"question" "q-104984" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-104984" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-104984" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-81164" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}y{e}^{x} & {e}^{x}-{e}^{y} \\\\ {e}^{x}-{e}^{y} & -x{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(1,1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $H(1,1)>0$\n- $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)>0$\n- The point $(1,1)$ is a local maximum of $f$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-81164" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=[\\begin{aligned}e & 0 \\\\ 0 & -e\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=det({f}^{″}(1,1))=e\\cdot (-e)-{0}^{2}=-{e}^{2}<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)=({f}^{″}(1,1))_{11}=e>0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(1,1)<0,$ the point $(1,1)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-81164" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a9fa983acf62cb4e9aa8bffc8cac999fd15910439357fbbe6843cbb905417b2c"} {"question" "q-81164" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-81164" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-81164" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-37588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function \n$f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x & -2y\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(0,0).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $H(0,0)>0$\n- $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)<0$\n- The point $(0,0)$ is a saddle point of $f$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-37588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x,\\,-2y\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x)=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y)=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=2\\cdot (-2)-{0}^{2}=-4<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,0))_{11}=2.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(0,0)<0,$ the point $(0,0)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-37588" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a9fa983acf62cb4e9aa8bffc8cac999fd15910439357fbbe6843cbb905417b2c"} {"question" "q-37588" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-37588" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of the statements are true"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-37588" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-37449" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function   $f(x,y)$\nhas the first derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y & 4x-2y\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(4,8).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $H(4,8)>0$\n- $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(4,8)>0$\n- The point $(4,8)$ is a saddle point of $f$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-37449" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y & 4x-2y\\end{aligned}],$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=4x-2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+4y)=-3{x}^{2}+4x \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4x-2y)=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-2y)=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}^{2}+4x & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(4,8),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(4,8)=[\\begin{aligned}-32 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(4,8),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(4,8)=det({f}^{″}(4,8))=(-32)\\cdot (-2)-{4}^{2}=48>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. We have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(4,8)=({f}^{″}(4,8))_{11}=-32<0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $H(4,8)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(4,8)<0,$ the point $(4,8)$ is a local maximum by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-37449" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a9fa983acf62cb4e9aa8bffc8cac999fd15910439357fbbe6843cbb905417b2c"} {"question" "q-37449" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-37449" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-37449" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-81043" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find and categorize all local the extrema of the function $f(x,y)={e}^{x}-x+{y}^{2},$ and determine which of the following statements is true." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-81043" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}-x+{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}-x+{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}-1 & 2y\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}-1=0 \\\\ 2y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n{e}^{x}-1=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n2y=0\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$  \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}-1)={e}^{x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}-1)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y)=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=1\\cdot 2-{0}^{2}=2>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,0))_{11}=1>0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-81043" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a9fa983acf62cb4e9aa8bffc8cac999fd15910439357fbbe6843cbb905417b2c"} {"question" "q-81043" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-81043" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(-1,1)$"] ["text" "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0)$"] ["text" "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,0)$"] ["text" "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(-1,0)$"] ["text" "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-81043" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0)$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-105281" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-y & 8{y}^{2}-x\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-105281" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-y & 8{y}^{2}-x\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={x}^{2}-y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=8{y}^{2}-x.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-y=0 \\\\ 8{y}^{2}-x=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-y=0\\,⟹\\,y={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{({x}^{2})}^{2}-x & =0 \\\\ 8{x}^{4}-x & =0 \\\\ x(8{x}^{3}-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $y={x}^{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\nx=0\\,\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,y={0}^{2}=0,\\,\\,x=\\frac{1}{2}\\,⟹\\,y={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ and $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-y)=2x\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(8{y}^{2}-x)=-1 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-y)=-1\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(8{y}^{2}-x)=16y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2x & -1 \\\\ -1 & 16y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))={0}^{2}-(-1)^{2}=-1<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})=det({f}^{″}(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}))=1\\cdot 4-(-1)^{2}=3>0\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4})={h}_{11}=1>0.$ Therefore, we have a local minimum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local minimum at $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{4}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256"