ion" "value" "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-{y}^{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x} & \\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{x}=0 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{y}{x}=0\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2(0)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x=0\\,⟹\\,x=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(1,0)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{y}{x})=-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y)=\\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{y}{x})=\\frac{1}{x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x-2y)=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} & \\frac{1}{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{x} & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,0)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(1,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,0)=det({f}^{″}(1,0))=0\\cdot (-2)-{1}^{2}=-1<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,0).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-105338" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a9fa983acf62cb4e9aa8bffc8cac999fd15910439357fbbe6843cbb905417b2c"} {"question" "q-105338" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-105338" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(1,0)$"] ["text" "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,0)$"] ["text" "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(e,0)$"] ["text" "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,0)$"] ["text" "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(e,0)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-105338" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,0)$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-105335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4-4y-4x & 4-4x+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-105335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4-4y-4x & 4-4x+{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=4-4y-4x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=4-4x+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4-4y-4x=0 \\\\ 4-4x+{y}^{2}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n4-4y-4x=0\\,⟹\\,x=1-y.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-4(1-y)+{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}+4y & =0 \\\\ y(y+4) & =0 \\\\ y & =-4,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting these values into $x=1-y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny=-4\\,⟹\\,x=1-(-4)=5,\\,\\,y=0\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,x=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(1,0)$ and $(5,-4)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-4y-4x)=-4\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4-4x+{y}^{2})=-4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-4y-4x)=-4\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4-4x+{y}^{2})=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2y\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(1,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,0)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(1,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,0)=det({f}^{″}(1,0))=(-4)\\cdot 0-(-4)^{2}=-16<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(1,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(5,-4),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(5,-4)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -8\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(5,-4),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(5,-4)=det({f}^{″}(5,-4))=(-4)\\cdot (-8)-(-4)^{2}=16>0\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(5,-4)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(5,-4)={h}_{11}=-4<0,$ the function $f$ has a local maximum at $(5,-4).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,0)$ and a local maximum at $(5,-4).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-105335" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "a9fa983acf62cb4e9aa8bffc8cac999fd15910439357fbbe6843cbb905417b2c"} {"question" "q-105335" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-105335" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,0)$ and a local maximum at $(5,-4)$"] ["text" "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,0)$ and a saddle point at $(5,-4)$"] ["text" "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,0)$ and a local maximum at $(5,-4)$"] ["text" "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(4,0)$ and a local maximum at $(3,1)$"] ["text" "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(4,0)$ and a local minimum at $(3,1)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "q-105335" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,0)$ and a local maximum at $(5,-4)$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/state.json" "file_sha256" "880a8c20fc5e36ca38af267fb3b1bade338c6cc298dc59f99dffa8f38bbe1df4"} {"question" "e-12584" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the critical points of $f(x,y) = x^3+y^3-3x^2y+9y.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/example-12584.json" "file_sha256" "943a8774f71c9c96050e919a7daf40886e1ec2ae1d3f3d45864db3c4abc42d6b"} {"question" "e-12584" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $\\mathbf a$ in the domain of $f$ such that $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) =\\mathbf 0^T$ or $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a)$ does not exist, where\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/db60be51a74c53caf3495a85e960be92ca8173c24c168e0ba30e335ef163be9e.png)\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is $\\mathbb{R}^2,$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =3{x}^{2}-6xy=3x(x-2y) \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =3{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/2faec0235882f18aa523890be6f7f52f446219ba9a6c27fe8f8179ae90050d0d.png)\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ such that $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T$ or $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T.$\n\nSetting $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T,$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x(x-2y)=0 \\\\ 3{y}^{2}-3{x}^{2}+9=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain $x=0$ or $x=2y.$ Now, we substitute these values into the second equation.\n\n- For $x=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n3y^2 - 3(0)^2 + 9 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad y^2 = -3,\n$$\n\nbut this last equation has no real solution.\n\n- For $x = 2y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n3y^2 - 3(2y)^2 + 9 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad y = \\pm 1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into $x = 2y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=-1 & \\,⟹\\,x=2(-1)=-2 \\\\ y=1 & \\,⟹\\,x=2(1)=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(-2,-1)$ and $(2,1).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has two critical points, $(-2,-1)$ and $(2,1).$ Note that since $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf 0^T$ at these two points, they are also both stationary points." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/example-12584.json" "file_sha256" "943a8774f71c9c96050e919a7daf40886e1ec2ae1d3f3d45864db3c4abc42d6b"} {"question" "e-12585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function \n$f(x,y) \n%= 2x + 2y - x^2 + y^2 + 2$\nhas the first derivative\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/083732de2321f70e27269ba55cec2a744a9424d443f68b4948caf313d30ae630.png)\nand has a stationary point at $(1,-1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $H(1,-1) > 0$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(1,-1) <    0$\n\n- The point $(1,-1)$ is a saddle point of $f$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/example-12585.json" "file_sha256" "c24066f8c76c6b98f55921430c3943666c6a3b278fa05a7b03e536f9e2681317"} {"question" "e-12585" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The second partial derivative test states that if $\\mathbf{a}\\in\\mathbb{R}^2$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(\\mathbf a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) > 0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) <                0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $\\mathbf{a}$\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) >0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $\\mathbf{a}$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) <       0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $\\mathbf{a}$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) = 0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/d680921af1b938e22a2705516c9f463cbb8b7bdb3ab1646b4613f6191e2a06f2.png) we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 2-2x, \n\\qquad \n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} =2+2y.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2-2x)=-2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2+2y)=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2+2y)=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,-1)=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of $\\boldsymbol f''(1,-1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,-1) = \\det(\\boldsymbol f''(1,-1)) = -2 \\cdot 2 - 0^2 = -4 < 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(1,-1) = (\\boldsymbol f''(1,-1))_{11} = -2 \\lt 0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(1,-1) < 0,$ the point $(1,-1)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/example-12585.json" "file_sha256" "c24066f8c76c6b98f55921430c3943666c6a3b278fa05a7b03e536f9e2681317"} {"question" "e-12586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/47070dbaf25d08b50c3f42c5f4ef9ce7d02b4e541112fa3049126f2d5cdcffed.png) \ngiven that\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/7e7f250d8444de28d127551ad1024b9b829d8ce8851cdb644efc69ad9469ce6d.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/example-12586.json" "file_sha256" "9a11096291c5e69a7ada3e54cd889a4567d10ba14fb99dea5ff20aa38873ae10"} {"question" "e-12586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The local extrema of ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/849819e826aeb3943d8aa6e489d270d71b85ed63f77c50f1b84ab0b93a399e1e.png) occurs at its critical points. A point ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/e604f9fa7e2d6f44f89ab4e5d557b403fdcd86b7203502d0d7