70288199f464c08b2b2cd7d4be1cd1b1ac67269db5fddbde.png) and  ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/e98c067307a4f26643da0e4f859761e543ab38ceea1e46a36bbe9f057edaca95.png) the function ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/f46326a9b32467a94ead9bda5846b0cf630f013472e283bf20d7260cbebf04a3.png) has a local minimum at ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/5e28ee39df67eea47e1396843ce2335220a81d287b2f0449532c7520bc4bf14d.png)\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/7e2b8d0dc4cd944605764e822a791a58cb95bdc4594f4f3058a55ab7fdcb3a01.png) we obtain\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/c0afb3bd05453415a4e4405d3204cf5f003e1b913dee6899507beb6ef55f6b97.png)\nComputing the determinant ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/706f896b329f2d7da4eb32dd48f7b3f81ab6b7d7b1dbc40181dcb9651fb94ff5.png) we get\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/dd7fd8d8eb625e1661dee2d7d418cf4be1c23f20a90932c4106d4b9b940709c4.png)\nTherefore, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/e505fe33cc7f6db13c021beaddcf71823fe506bc914c45c8c24520d905b1c8d7.png) is a saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/557ab1b22319727accc0f327cc1981a28de2f44f4e6c4cba8c55fc9863b3cc81.png) has a local minimum at ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/6f22d6fba010e28fb4e39bbeb1d228d2d5fce35b324d55bdf0c209a983f7d970.png) and a saddle point at ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/assets/6aed8e1c686dfe44296c0f23dd69bb4442ad30864f5600c63b0ceff16da7b576.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070654/example-12586.json" "file_sha256" "9a11096291c5e69a7ada3e54cd889a4567d10ba14fb99dea5ff20aa38873ae10"}] "usage" {"q-36622" [] "e-12584" [] "e-12585" [] "e-12586" [] "q-37588" []} "basis" 2499 "retractions" [[17592186124920 :question/problem "Determine the stationary points of $f(x, y) = 2\\ln x - 2xy + y^{2}$."] [17592186124997 :question/problem "The function\n$f(x, y)$\nhas the first derivative\n$f^{′}(x, y) = \\begin{bmatrix}2x & -2y\\end{bmatrix}$,\nand has a stationary point at $(0, 0)$. If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $H(0, 0) > 0$\n2. $\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}}(0, 0) < 0$\n3. The point $(0, 0)$ is a saddle point of $f$"] [17592186124134 :question/problem "Determine the critical points of $f(x,y) = x^3+y^3-3x^2y+9y$."] [17592186124134 :question/worked-solution "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $\\mathbf a$ in the domain of $f$ such that\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a) =\\mathbf 0^T\n$$\n\nor $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂x_{n}}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is $\\mathbb{R}^2$, and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x} &= 3x^{2} - 6xy = 3x(x - 2y) \\\\\n\\frac{∂f}{∂y} &= 3y^{2} - 3x^{2} + 9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3x(x - 2y) & 3y^{2} - 3x^{2} + 9\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ such that\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T\n$$\n\nor $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T\n$$\n\nSetting\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T\n$$\n\nwe have the following system:\n\n$$\n{3x(x - 2y) = 0; 3y^{2} - 3x^{2} + 9 = 0\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain $x=0$ or $x=2y$. Now, we substitute these values into the second equation.\n\n- For $x=0$, we get\n$3y^2 - 3(0)^2 + 9 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad y^2 = -3$,\nbut this last equation has no real solution.\n- For $x = 2y$, we obtain\n$3y^2 - 3(2y)^2 + 9 = 0 \\qquad \\Longrightarrow \\qquad y = \\pm 1$.\nSubstituting these values into $x = 2y$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}y =-1 & ⟹x = 2(-1) =-2 \\\\ y = 1 & ⟹x = 2(1) = 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(-2,-1)$ and $(2,1)$.\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has two critical points, $(-2,-1)$ and $(2,1)$. Note that since\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf 0^T\n$$\n\nat these two points, they are also both stationary points."] [17592186124135 :question/problem "The function\n$f(x,y) %= 2x + 2y - x^2 + y^2 + 2$\nhas the first derivative\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 - 2x & 2 + 2y\\end{bmatrix}$\nand has a stationary point at $(1,-1)$. If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $H(1,-1) > 0$\n2. $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(1,-1) < 0$\n3. The point $(1,-1)$ is a saddle point of $f$"] [17592186124135 :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $\\mathbf{a}\\in\\mathbb{R}^2$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(\\mathbf a)$, we have the following:\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) > 0$, then:if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) < 0$, then $f$ has a local maximum at $\\mathbf{a}$ if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) >0$, then $f$ has a local minimum at $\\mathbf{a}$\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) < 0$, then $f$ has a saddle point at $\\mathbf{a}$\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) = 0$, then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we notice that since $\\begin{bmatrix}2 - 2x & 2 + 2y\\end{bmatrix}$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 2-2x, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} =2+2y\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 - 2x) =-2 \\\\\n\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y^{2}} &= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2 + 2y) = 2 \\\\\n\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y} &= \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y∂x} = \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2 + 2y) = 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(x, y) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y^{2}}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,-1)$, we obtain\n\n$$\nf^{″}(1,-1) = \\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of $\\boldsymbol f''(1,-1)$, we get\n$H(1,-1) = \\det(\\boldsymbol f''(1,-1)) = -2 \\cdot 2 - 0^2 = -4 < 0$.\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(1,-1) = (\\boldsymbol f''(1,-1))_{11} = -2 \\lt 0$.\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(1,-1) < 0$, the point $(1,-1)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186124136 :question/problem "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function\n$f(x,y) % = x^3 -6xy + y^3$\ngiven that\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3x^{2} - 3 & 2y\\end{bmatrix}$"] [17592186124136 :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occurs at its critical points. A point $\\mathbf{a}$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf{a}) = \\mathbf{0}^T\n$$\n\n(for stationary points) or $\\boldsymbol f'(\\mathbf{a})$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $\\mathbf{a}\\in\\mathbb{R}^2$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(\\mathbf a)$, we have the following:\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) > 0$, then:if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) < 0$, then $f$ has a local maximum at $\\mathbf{a}$ if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) >0$, then $f$ has a local minimum at $\\mathbf{a}$\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) < 0$, then $f$ has a saddle point at $\\mathbf{a}$\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) = 0$, then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since $\\begin{bmatrix}3x^{2} - 3 & 2y\\end{bmatrix}$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 3x^2 - 3, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} =2y\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting\n\n$$\n\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T\n$$\n\nwe obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n{3x^{2} - 3 = 0; 2y = 0\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x$, we get\n\n$$\n3x^2 -3= 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad x=\\pm 1\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y$, we get\n\n$$\n2y = 0 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad y=0\n$$\n\nHence, $(1,0)$ and $(-1,0)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y)$.\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}} &= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x^{2} - 3) = 6x & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y) = 0 \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(3x^{2} - 3) = 0 & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y^{2}}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y) = 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{″}(x, y) &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x^{2}} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{∂^{2}f}{∂x∂y}\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}6x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(1,0)$, we obtain\n$f^{″}(1, 0) = \\begin{bmatrix}6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\nComputing the determinant $H(1,0)$, we get\n$H(1,0) = \\det(\\boldsymbol f''(1,0)) = 6 \\cdot 2 - 0^2 = 12 > 0$.\nNow, since $H(1,0) > 0$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(1,0) = (\\boldsymbol f''(1,0))_{11}= 6>0$, the function $f$ has a local minimum at $(1,0)$.\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(-1,0)$, we obtain\n$f^{″}(-1, 0) = \\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$.\nComputing the determinant $H(-1,0)$, we get\n$H(-1,0) = \\det(\\boldsymbol f''(-1,0)) = -6\\cdot 2 - 0^2 = -12 < 0$\nTherefore, $f(-1,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(1,0)$ and a saddle point at $(-1,0)$."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186122543 17592186122544 17592186122545 17592186122546 17592186122547 17592186123005 17592186123006 17592186123007 17592186123008 17592186123009] "immutable_fields" [[17592186122542 :answer-field/correct] [17592186122542 :answer-field/id] [17592186122542 :answer-field/key] [17592186122542 :answer-field/type] [17592186123004 :answer-field/correct] [17592186123004 :answer-field/id] [17592186123004 :answer-field/key] [17592186123004 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
